to extend this application into a beginner-friendly DSA learning platform.\n\nMy goal is to learn Data Structures and Algorithms from the fundamentals, practice curated LeetCode problems, understand solution patterns, visualize Python execution, and track my progress.\n\n2. Non-Negotiable Requirements\n\nDo not rebuild my application from scratch.\n\nDo not replace or rewrite my existing execution engine.\n\nDo not modify the original Python code used by the existing visualizer.\n\nPreserve all existing working features, animations, navigation, and explanations.\n\nDo not introduce OpenAI, Gemini, Claude, or any other AI APIs.\n\nDo not require API keys, paid services, cloud databases, authentication, or mandatory internet access.\n\nUse Python as the only supported programming language initially.\n\nStore learning progress locally on my computer.\n\nReuse existing components, dependencies, and architecture wherever possible.\n\nInspect the project before making changes.\n\nMake incremental changes and test them before proceeding.\n\n3. Phase 1: Create the DSA Learning Dashboard\n\nAdd a new navigation section called \"DSA Learning\".\n\nBuild a clean, beginner-friendly dashboard that matches the existing application's design.\n\nInclude:\n\nTotal problems in the curated library.\n\nProblems solved.\n\nProblems attempted.\n\nProblems not started.\n\nBookmarked problems.\n\nTopic-wise progress.\n\nDifficulty-wise progress.\n\nContinue Learning.\n\nRecently practiced problems.\n\nA beginner learning roadmap.\n\nUse actual saved data for progress statistics. Do not generate fake completion numbers or streaks.\n\nInitially include 300 curated, distinct DSA problems. These must be real, identifiable problems, not 300 generated titles or duplicated entries.\n\n5. 4. Phase 2: Organize Problems by Topic\n\nCreate a searchable topic library covering:\n\nArrays\n\nStrings\n\nHashing and Hash Maps\n\nTwo Pointers\n\nSliding Window\n\nPrefix Sum\n\nBinary Search\n\nSorting\n\nLinked Lists\n\nStacks\n\nQueues and Deques\n\nRecursion\n\nBacktracking\n\nTrees and Binary Trees\n\nBinary Search Trees\n\nHeaps and Priority Queues\n\nGraphs\n\nBreadth-First Search (BFS)\n\nDepth-First Search (DFS)\n\nGreedy Algorithms\n\nDynamic Programming\n\nBit Manipulation\n\nTries\n\nUnion-Find\n\nTopological Sorting\n\nIntervals\n\nMonotonic Stack and Queue\n\nMath and Number Theory\n\nMatrix Problems\n\nAdvanced Data Structures\n\nProblems may belong to multiple topics.\n\nEach topic page should display its problem count, difficulty distribution, completion progress, and recommended next problem.\n\nAllow users to browse by topic, difficulty, algorithmic pattern, and learning status.\n6. 5. Phase 3: Create a Curated 300-Problem Library\n\nBuild a structured catalog of 300 curated DSA problems.\n\nInclude a sensible distribution across beginner, intermediate, and advanced topics. Do not force equal numbers into every category.\n\nPrioritize a learning progression:\n\nPython fundamentals and basic array operations.\n\nStrings, hashing, and frequency counting.\n\nTwo pointers and sliding windows.\n\nPrefix sums and binary search.\n\nSorting, linked lists, stacks, and queues.\n\nRecursion and backtracking.\n\nTrees, heaps, and graphs.\n\nGreedy algorithms and dynamic programming.\n\nAdvanced patterns and data structures.\n\nEach problem must have a structured record containing:\n\nUnique problem ID.\n\nProblem title.\n\nVerified source URL, preferably the official LeetCode page.\n\nDifficulty.\n\nPrimary topic.\n\nAdditional topic tags.\n\nAlgorithmic pattern.\n\nShort original problem summary.\n\nInput and output examples.\n\nConstraints, where verified.\n\nPrerequisite concepts.\n\nLearning status.\n\nBookmark status.\n\nPersonal notes.\n\nOptional Python reference solution.\n\nVerification status for the reference solution.\n\nDo not invent problem URLs, difficulty labels, constraints, or company tags.\n\nUse public, reliable sources to validate the catalog where possible. Do not claim the library is exhaustive or fully verified if it is not.\n\nDo not copy the complete copyrighted text of LeetCode problem statements. Write concise original summaries and examples, and link to the official problem page.\n\nIf 300 verified records cannot be completed in one implementation pass, create a data-driven catalog and import the remaining verified records in batches. Do not fill gaps with fake problems.\n\nThe application must remain functional when external links are unavailable.\n7. 6. Phase 4: Add Problem Search and Filters\n\nImplement functional search and filtering.\n\nUsers must be able to filter by:\n\nTopic.\n\nDifficulty: Easy, Medium, Hard.\n\nStatus: Not Started, Attempted, Solved, Review.\n\nAlgorithmic pattern.\n\nBookmarked status.\n\nAdd sorting by title, difficulty, and learning order.\n\nProvide pagination or efficient list rendering so the catalog remains responsive.\n\nEach problem should have a unique route or detail view. Prevent duplicate records when importing additional problems.\n8. 7. Phase 5: Create a Problem Learning Workspace\n\nWhen a user opens a problem, display a dedicated learning page.\n\nA. Understand the Problem\n\nExplain the problem in simple English.\n\nShow:\n\nWhat the problem asks.\n\nInput and output examples.\n\nExpected output for each example.\n\nImportant constraints.\n\nEdge cases.\n\nWhat the learner needs to calculate or return.\n\nDo not immediately reveal the solution.\n\nB. Learn the Pattern\n\nExplain:\n\nWhich DSA pattern is relevant.\n\nWhy the pattern is useful.\n\nWhich prerequisite concepts are needed.\n\nA small illustrative example.\n\nA simple explanation of the intended approach.\n\nUse built-in, rule-based teaching content. Do not call an AI API.\n\nC. Try It Yourself\n\nProvide a Python editor where the user can write their own solution.\n\nInclude:\n\nRun Code.\n\nRun Tests.\n\nReset Code.\n\nShow Hints.\n\nReveal Reference Solution, with confirmation.\n\nView Test Results.\n\nUse a safe, restricted execution environment. Never execute unrestricted user code on the main application server.\n\nIf the existing execution infrastructure cannot safely support arbitrary Python, implement a restricted interpreter for supported syntax or clearly document the limitation.\n\nD. Visualize Execution\n\nIntegrate with my existing Code Flow Visualizer.\n\nWhen supported Python code is submitted for visualization:\n\nDisplay the actual executing line.\n\nTrack variable values.\n\nExplain conditions and loops.\n\nShow execution history.\n\nSupport Previous Step and Next Step.\n\nPreserve existing animations and beginner explanations.\n\nDo not create a duplicate execution engine unnecessarily.\n\nIf the existing visualizer cannot handle a particular Python construct, explain which construct is unsupported. Never fabricate execution steps or results.\n\n9. 8. Phase 6: Add Python Reference Solutions and Tests\n\nFor curated problems, provide optional reference solutions in Python.\n\nEvery solution must be reviewed and tested against appropriate examples and edge cases before being marked verified.\n\nWhere applicable, include:\n\nA straightforward baseline approach.\n\nAn optimized approach.\n\nTime complexity.\n\nSpace complexity.\n\nExplanation of the algorithm.\n\nCommon mistakes.\n\nWhy the chosen approach works.\n\nDo not provide multiple solutions for every problem if doing so reduces solution quality.\n\nKeep solutions hidden by default to encourage independent practice.\n\nUse tests to validate correctness rather than relying on explanations alone.\n\n10. 9. Phase 7: Build a Beginner Roadmap\n\nCreate an optional structured learning roadmap.\n\nSuggested order:\n\nStage 1: Python fundamentals and arrays.\n\nStage 2: Strings and hashing.\n\nStage 3: Two pointers and sliding windows.\n\nStage 4: Prefix sums, sorting, and binary search.\n\nStage 5: Linked lists, stacks, and queues.\n\nStage 6: Recursion and backtracking.\n\nStage 7: Trees and binary search trees.\n\nStage 8: Heaps and graphs.\n\nStage 9: Greedy algorithms and dynamic programming.\n\nStage 10: Advanced patterns and data structures.\n\nLet users skip topics, revisit concepts, and select their own learning order.\n\nDo not mark a topic as mastered just because a user opened a problem.\n\nUse meaningful completion rules, such as passing tests and marking a problem solved.\n\n<summary>\n1. **Task Overview**\n   - **User Request**: Implement Phase 7: Build a Beginner Roadmap inside the existing \"Teach Me This Code\" DSA platform.\n   - **Specific Stage Requirements (Exact 10-Stage Learning Order)**:\n     - Stage 1: Python fundamentals and arrays.\n     - Stage 2: Strings and hashing.\n     - Stage 3: Two pointers and sliding windows.\n     - Stage 4: Prefix sums, sorting, and binary search.\n     - Stage 5: Linked lists, stacks, and queues.\n     - Stage 6: Recursion and backtracking.\n     - Stage 7: Trees and binary search trees.\n     - Stage 8: Heaps and graphs.\n     - Stage 9: Greedy algorithms and dynamic programming.\n     - Stage 10: Advanced patterns and data structures.\n   - **Key Functional Rules**:\n     - Freedom to skip topics, revisit concepts, and select custom learning order (stages are not locked or gated).\n     - Do not mark a topic as mastered merely because a user opened a problem or viewed a page.\n     - Use meaningful completion rules: real solved count (`progress.solved`), passing tests, and milestone problem completion.\n   - **Non-Negotiables**:\n     - Do not rebuild from scratch; preserve existing execution engine (`engine.js`), explanations, animations, and navigation.\n     - Kadane maximum subarray verified output must remain 6 for `[-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]`.\n     - Zero AI APIs (no OpenAI, Gemini, Claude, paid services, cloud DBs). Offline rule-based content and client-side safe execution only.\n     - Store progress locally in `localStorage`.\n\n2. **Progress**\n   - **Phase 6 Completed